  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Hi, doctor. Well, honestly, it’s been a long time coming, I think. I manage a health clinic—I'm a medical services manager—and, you know, at fifty, you'd think I'd have my workflow completely dialed in by now. I've been doing this kind of work for a while, and I try, I really do. I have my desk set up, I have my schedule blocked out, but... well, what finally pushed me to actually make this appointment and come in today is that I just cannot seem to hold my focus on the things I absolutely have to do to keep the clinic running smoothly. \n\nFor example, I’ll sit down at my computer to start an EPIC quality report, which is a huge part of my job, or I’ll go into one of our weekly clinic metrics meetings with the department heads. And I go in with every intention of tracking the data, paying close attention to the compliance numbers, really being present and engaged. But within... I don't know, just a few minutes, my mind has drifted so far away. It’s like I’m physically there, but mentally I'm completely gone, thinking about something completely unrelated. \n\nI'll catch myself rereading the exact same column of numbers on my screen over and over again without absorbing a single thing, or suddenly I'll \"wake up\" in a meeting and realize I’ve missed the entire point of the discussion the physicians were just having. And it’s not just once in a while. I’m spending hours on reports that should take thirty minutes just because I have to keep backtracking. It’s incredibly frustrating because I know how important these metrics are for our funding and our patient care, and I just sit there thinking, 'why can't I just pay attention like everyone else in the room?' It’s exhausting, to be honest, just trying to keep my head above water, and I felt like I couldn't keep hiding it or working around it anymore.",
