    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back to your childhood and school years, did you struggle with your focus and attention back then as well?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, absolutely. Yes, it goes way, way back. It’s funny you ask that, because I was actually just talking to my sister about this the other day, and we were reminiscing, well, if you can call it that, about how much of a struggle school was for me. I mean, I wasn't failing or anything—I always managed to scrape by because I was smart enough to figure things out at the last minute—but it was never easy. \n\nEven in elementary school, I vividly remember the comments on my report cards. It was always the same thing, year after year. The teachers would write these little notes, you know, in that neat cursive they all seemed to have, saying things like, \"She's very bright, but she's so careless,\" or \"She has so much potential if she would just apply herself and pay attention.\" I hated those comments. It made me feel like I was doing something wrong on purpose, but I wasn't! I just... my brain wouldn't stay put. \n\nAnd the homework... oh, the homework was a nightmare. My poor mother. She practically had to tether herself to me at the kitchen table every single night just to make sure I got it done. If she walked away for five minutes to stir a pot on the stove or answer the phone, I’d be off in my own world, staring at a spot on the wall or playing with my pencil eraser, completely oblivious to the math worksheet sitting right in front of me. She had to constantly redirect me. And that’s if I even brought the homework home in the first place! Half the time, I’d completely lose track of what the assignments even were, or I'd leave the textbook I needed sitting right in my desk at school. I’d get all the way home, sit down, and realize I didn't have the one book I needed to answer the questions. It drove my parents crazy, and honestly, it drove me crazy too, even back then. I just always felt like I was scrambling to keep up with things that seemed so effortless for the other kids. So, yes, it’s definitely not a new problem, it's just... well, it feels like the stakes are so much higher now with my career.",
