    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Aside from the frustration with your focus, have you been experiencing a persistent feeling of sadness or a depressed mood?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "No, no, I wouldn't say I've been feeling sad or depressed. I mean, I know what that looks like, working in healthcare, you see a lot of depression, and I really don't think that's what's going on with me. It’s not a heavy, dark cloud kind of feeling, you know? I still enjoy my weekends, I love spending time with my family, I like going out to dinner... I don't have that sense of hopelessness or anything like that. \n\nThe primary emotion I feel is just... intense frustration. And maybe a bit of anxiety, but only because I'm constantly worried I'm going to drop the ball at work. Like, if I'm sitting there and I know I have a deadline for a compliance audit, and I just can't get my brain to engage with the spreadsheet, I get so mad at myself. I'll think, \"Come on, just do it, it's right in front of you!\" But it's not sadness. It's more like being trapped in a car where the engine is running but the transmission won't engage. \n\nI guess sometimes the frustration can feel a little overwhelming, and maybe someone looking from the outside might mistake that for being down, because I’ll come home from the clinic just totally drained. I'll collapse on the couch and tell my husband I'm exhausted, but it's an exhaustion from fighting my own brain all day, not an exhaustion from life in general, if that makes sense. I still want to do things, I still have goals, I still care about my job—probably too much, honestly, which is why this is bothering me so much. But no, a persistent sadness? No, I definitely wouldn't say I have that. It's really just the focus and the mental drifting that are driving me up a wall.",
