    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Do you find yourself worrying excessively about a variety of everyday matters, like health, finances, or family, beyond just your work performance?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Well, no, not really. I mean, I worry about things, sure, because who doesn't? But it's not this constant, overwhelming dread about everything in my life. Like, I don't sit up at night agonizing over our retirement accounts or stressing that my husband is going to get into a car accident on his way to work. I'm pretty practical about that kind of stuff. I check our finances, they look fine, I move on. \n\nAnd health-wise, being a clinic manager, you'd think I'd be a massive hypochondriac with all the things I see, but I'm really not. I know what the red flags are, I get my mammograms, I get my blood work done, and unless my doctor tells me something is wrong, I don't give it a second thought. I don't spiral into thinking every headache is a brain tumor, you know? \n\nEven with my family, my kids are grown and out of the house, and they're doing well. One's an engineer, the other's in grad school, and I'm incredibly proud of them. I might worry a little if they call me sounding stressed, but it's a normal mom worry, not this consuming anxiety. \n\nIt always, always comes back to work. That's where the anxiety lives. It's the fear of dropping the ball because my brain decided to take a vacation during a crucial meeting. Like last Tuesday, I had this moment of sheer panic because I couldn't remember if I had actually submitted the quarterly staffing report, or if I had just dreamed that I submitted it while I was staring blankly at my monitor for twenty minutes. I had to log back in from home at 8 PM just to check, and my heart was pounding the whole time. But that's all tied to my inability to just sit down and focus. If I didn't have this constant battle with my own attention span, I really don't think I'd be an anxious person at all. I'm actually a very organized, capable person by nature... it's just that my brain refuses to cooperate with my own systems! So no, I wouldn't say I have excessive worry about everyday life stuff, just the work things that I know I'm messing up.",
